---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

// Keep this in sync with the in-app changelog
// (badger-fit: lib/features/about/domain/changelog.dart) and the store
// release notes. Newest first.
const releases = [
  {
    version: '1.1.2',
    date: 'July 2026',
    headline: 'Search your notes, and a Plan B for busy gyms',
    highlights: [
      'Search everything you have ever written down. The search icon on History looks across workout notes, set comments, exercise and machine notes, routine notes, and your training notes, and takes you straight to whatever you tap. It searches your writing, not the app\'s, and none of it leaves your device.',
      'Plan B: when a machine is taken, the training screen\'s menu suggests substitutes on the spot. They share a primary muscle with the lift you are replacing, and machine exercises only show up if that machine is actually at your gym. Works in a free workout too, not just a routine.',
      'A Pace section on the workout summary shows how long each exercise took and how long you rested between its sets, worked out from your own log. Nothing extra to tap while you train. A gap over ten minutes counts as a break rather than rest, so a phone call does not skew your averages.',
      'Type a duration as a time. "15:00" is fifteen minutes, and a plain number is still seconds, so nothing you already do changes. Works everywhere Badger asks for a duration.',
      'The exercise page now leads with your history for that movement. The record key at the top taps straight through to the graph, and muscles worked and your notes sit in sections you can open when you want them.',
      'The machine picker lists everything else at your gym under the ones already marked compatible. Pick one and Badger links it to the exercise for you, so there is nothing to set up in advance. Creating a machine or attachment now links it to your gyms up front and lands you on its page to finish.',
      'Trained on this date a year ago? A quiet line at the bottom of Today says so, and opens that session.',
      'Share files carry more of a routine: supersets, tempo, and exercise alternatives now travel with it. Bar weights go across as the number you typed with its unit, instead of being converted to kg on the way out. Android: tapping a .badger file you downloaded now opens it in Badger.',
      'A 5-day Push/Pull/Legs and Upper/Lower split joins the built-in programs. New installs get it in the list; if you already have Badger, download it from the program gallery on badger.fit.',
      'Sets you planned but never performed no longer count as done. Your monthly report, heatmap, and muscle coverage now count only what you actually logged, so if you have abandoned sessions in your history some of those numbers will drop. The smaller number is the true one.',
      'Fixes: a crash on days holding more than one workout, an exercise name squeezed off the screen by its illustration, an exercise setting that showed "lbs" over a kg number, and a default weight increment that drifted from 2.5 kg to 2.27 every time you saved. Workout CSV exports gain is_complete and is_skipped columns, and importing from Strong, Stronglifts, or Hevy now brings your session start and end times across.',
    ],
  },
  {
    version: '1.1.1',
    date: 'July 2026',
    headline: 'Rep ranges, and a library you can scan',
    highlights: [
      'Set a rep range on a routine target, like 8-10 instead of a single number. Anywhere in the range meets the plan, and once you reach the top Badger suggests adding weight. Double progression, built in.',
      'The exercise library is now one A-Z list with a letter rail down the side and search that filters as you type. Categories moved into the filter row, with a Manage categories screen for everything else.',
      'Android: a notification keeps your current exercise and next target on the lock screen for the whole workout, and shows the rest countdown while it runs. Tap it to jump back in.',
      'Add a photo to any machine or attachment, from the camera or your gallery, so you can recognize it in the picker.',
      'Android: a home-screen widget showing your week, your last entry, and your next routine day.',
      'A Training notes page under You for your own notes, plus a roomier notes editor on every routine.',
      'Settings are regrouped into Logging, Coaching, App, and Your data.',
    ],
  },
  {
    version: '1.1.0',
    date: 'July 2026',
    headline: 'Badger looks like a training log now',
    highlights: [
      'A complete redesign: paper and slate, ruled entries instead of cards, and tally marks for your sets. It reads like a field journal.',
      'Your set progress now inks in as you complete each set, right above the set list.',
      'Every exercise row leads with its equipment mark, so you can find a lift by shape as much as by name.',
      'Personal records are marked with a flag you can spot at a glance, and a stamp on the summary when you set an all-time best.',
      'Muscle maps throughout: what an exercise works, what a routine covers, and where your volume actually went.',
      'History reads as entries: tap one to open it, with the week at a glance up top.',
      'Charts redrawn on graph paper, with your all-time best marked as a line to beat.',
      'Rest timer on the iPhone lock screen and Dynamic Island.',
    ],
  },
  {
    version: '1.0.12',
    date: 'July 2026',
    headline: 'Supersets everywhere, and records for every lift',
    highlights: [
      'Redesigned supersets: create a group once, edit it in one place, and set it up the same way in a live workout, a routine, or past history.',
      'Personal records now work for every exercise type, not just weight × reps - timed holds, distances, reps-only, and more.',
      'On iPhone, the rest timer now shows on your lock screen and Dynamic Island.',
      'Workout summaries compare what you planned against what you actually lifted.',
      'Configurable warm-up set generator with per-exercise overrides.',
    ],
  },
  {
    version: '1.0.11',
    date: 'June 2026',
    highlights: [
      'Fixed a unit bug where lbs users could see weights in kg.',
      'Deload weeks no longer carry their reduced weights into the next session.',
      'Log a past workout from the calendar, with a timer-free backdated flow.',
    ],
  },
  {
    version: '1.0.10',
    date: 'May 2026',
    highlights: [
      'Editable history: tap any logged set to correct it after the fact.',
      'Machine-scoped exercise notes and per-machine weight configuration.',
      'Share gyms, machines, and routines as .badger files.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Changelog"
  description="What's new in Badger - a release-by-release list of features, improvements, and fixes."
>
  <Header currentPath={Astro.url.pathname} />

  <main class="doc">
    <span class="kicker">Release log</span>
    <h1>What's new</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every release of Badger, newest first. Badger is in active development on Android and in beta
      on iOS.
    </p>

    {
      releases.map((r, i) => (
        <article class="release">
          <div class="release-head">
            <h2>Version {r.version}</h2>
            {i === 0 && <span class="release-latest">Latest</span>}
            <span class="release-date">{r.date}</span>
          </div>

          {r.headline && <p class="release-headline">{r.headline}</p>}

          <ul class="release-notes">
            {r.highlights.map((h) => (
              <li>{h}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .doc {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 60px 24px 0;
  }

  .kicker {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .doc h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 38px;
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .lede {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 56ch;
  }

  /* Each release is a journal entry, the same shape the promise page uses: a 2px
     ink rule opens it, and the card it used to live in is gone. */
  .release {
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding-top: 20px;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .release-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 10px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* The version is the entry's heading, so it takes the serif. The numerals in
     it are part of a title, not data being compared down a column. */
  .release-head h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .release-latest {
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  .release-date {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .release-headline {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .release-notes {
    margin: 10px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .release-notes li {
    padding: 9px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .doc {
      padding: 44px 20px 0;
    }

    .doc h1 {
      font-size: 30px;
    }

    /* The date has no room to sit opposite the version at phone width. */
    .release-date {
      margin-left: 0;
      flex-basis: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
